<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Schönborn family</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as House of Schönborn</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Schönborn are a German noble family from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a> who rose to great power in the church in the 17th and 18th centuries. Members of the family were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> and prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a>, Speyer and Constance. They were great builders, commissioning baroque palaces such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg-residence">Würzburg Residence</a>, Pommersfelden and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruchsal">Bruchsal</a> from architects including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balthasar-neumann">Balthasar Neumann</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The family first appears in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rheingau">Rheingau</a> with H. von Sconenburne in 1275, its original seat being Schönborn in the County of Katzenelnbogen. By the end of the 14th century it had split into branches, one serving as Burgmann at the castle of Burgschwalbach and the other as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westerwald">Westerwald</a>. In the 16th century so many younger sons became cathedral canons that both branches died out, except a side line holding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> of Freienfels and Eschbach. From it came Johann Philipp von Schönborn, son of a minor nobleman in the service of the Lutheran counts of Wied, who became a Catholic priest during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Johann Philipp was elected prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a> in 1642 and Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> in 1647, which made him an elector and archchancellor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. He suppressed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> in his lands and helped mediate the peace of 1648. His brother Philip Erwein acquired castles and estates and became a baron in 1663, and from his twelve children the later lines descend. Philip Erwein's son Lothar Franz became prince-bishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a> in 1693 and Elector of Mainz in 1695. In 1726 Emperor Charles VI granted him Palanok Castle and Mukacheve with 200 villages in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, one of the largest estates in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>.</p><p>Through the marriage of Rudolf Franz to Eleonore von Hatzfeld in 1701 the family gained Wiesentheid and, for the first time, imperial immediacy. Its counties were divided in 1717 and mediatised in 1806. In 1743 members of the family ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstanz">Konstanz</a>, Speyer, Worms and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>. Their rise rested on elections by cathedral chapters across generations, backed by advantageous marriages; the family never gained hereditary princely rank. At the end of the 18th century three brothers founded the Austrian, Franconian and Bohemian branches that still exist.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Schönborn prelates were among the main builders of Southern German baroque, employing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-dientzenhofer">Johann Dientzenhofer</a>, Maximilian von Welsch and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-lukas-von-hildebrandt">Johann Lukas von Hildebrandt</a> as well as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balthasar-neumann">Balthasar Neumann</a>. More than 100 churches were built under their rule, among them the Basilica of the Fourteen Holy Helpers. The years 1642 to 1756 are called the Schönbornzeit, a name now given to a style of Rhenish and Franconian baroque. Weissenstein Palace at Pommersfelden, still owned by the family, holds the largest private baroque art collection in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “House of Schönborn” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the <b>Schönborn</b> bishops Balthasar Neumann built the Residenz, decorated with Tiepolo's ceiling frescoes, and vineyards along the Main supported a wealthy wine trade.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-wurzburg">Prince-Bishopric of Würzburg</a></li></ul></section>
